# Smokin rules — the substrate that survives its own orchestrator

One copy. Every `TASK.md` references this file rather than restating it; if a task's rules and
this file disagree, **this file wins and one of them needs fixing.**

Smokin is Grillin's third substrate. Grillin decides *what the plan is*; Smokin *runs it*. The two
are separate on purpose — a plan that cannot be read by a human is not improved by being run by a
machine.

---

## 0 · Confirm the tick is the orchestrator

```bash
smokin doctor .
```

There is **no long-running orchestrator process.** `smokin tick` reads the plan off disk,
dispatches what is ready, reaps what finished, renders the surface, and exits. Everything it needs
is a file in this directory.

That is the whole claim, and it is the one Grillin's principle 14 has always demanded and never
had: *assume amnesia — anything living only in a conversation is already gone.* Compaction,
`Ctrl-C`, an SSH drop, `herdr server stop`, closing the terminal — all of them are no-ops on the
plan, because the plan's state was never in the process.

**Test it rather than believing it:** `kill -9` a worker, close the terminal, open a new one, run
`smokin tick` once. Same frontier, the dead worker reaped, the next wave dispatched.

---

## 1 · The routing rule — pane or in-process

Declared per task, first match wins:

| # | If | Then |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `**Interrupt:** yes` | **PANE** — it has an approval-bearing or human-answerable step |
| 2 | `**Watch:** yes` | **PANE** — somebody stated in writing they will look at it |
| 3 | `**Type:** ASK` | **PANE** — an ASK node terminates on a human |
| 4 | `**Budget:** > 900` | **PANE** — work that outlives the tick must outlive its process |
| 5 | the runtime has no headless mode | **PANE** — the only way to run it at all |
| 6 | otherwise | **INPROC** |

**The rule points the opposite way from the reflex. You do not open a pane to get parallelism.**
Parallelism is free in-process — the tick forks and reaps. A pane is bought with a screen and paid
for in human review capacity, and that is the one constraint no tool in the prior art solved.

**Honest limit.** Clauses 3 and 5 have an external referent. `Interrupt`, `Watch` and `Budget` are
author declarations, so a gate on them checks *self-consistency*, not truth. Dispatch is
author-chosen inside a checked envelope; one clause of six is pure taste, declared where a reader
can argue with it. Anyone who tells you the routing is a pure function has not tried to flip one.

---

## 2 · The receipt is a claim. The verdict is the project's.

| File | Written by | Says |
|---|---|---|
| `RECEIPT.json` | the worker's wrapper or hook | *the agent claims it finished* |
| `VERDICT.json` | the tick, re-running the task's **own** `## Done means` | *a second hand checked* |

**A receipt may be a precondition of a gate. It may never BE the gate.** A receipt plus a two-line
findings file passes `test -f` with zero real work done — which would turn Grillin's most
load-bearing check into a liveness probe. This is principle 8 one level down: nobody certifies
their own work, including a worker certifying its own completion.

`PROGRESS.md` renders `● verified` and `◑ claimed, ungated` differently for exactly this reason.

---

## 3 · The spool

```
.smokin/dispatch/<ID>.json   written BEFORE launch
.smokin/spool/{tmp,inbox,done}
tasks/<ID>/RECEIPT.json      published tmp → fsync → rename
```

**The dispatch record is what makes silence mean something.** Without it, a cold reader looking at
a task with no receipt cannot tell *never dispatched* from *running right now* from *was running
when the machine died*.

Three rules that are not negotiable because each one is a defect that was found:

- **Publish by `rename(2)`, never in place.** Two writers per task are guaranteed — the wrapper's
  trap and a native hook — and a stat is not a mutex.
- **`seq` is allocated once, at dispatch.** `<run>:<task>:<attempt>`. Derive it from a timestamp
  and duplicate emissions carry different keys, so the dedup never collides.
- **Claim before acting.** `rename(inbox → done)` *then* act. Acting first leaves a window where a
  crash re-dispatches dependants on top of still-running copies.

`spool/tmp` and `spool/inbox` must be on one filesystem, and so must a task folder and its own
receipt. `smokin doctor` checks it.

---

## 4 · The presenter — the plan has to be visible to a human

**A process nobody can see is a process that gets missed.** Two files, and the split between them
is the rule:

| | |
|---|---|
| `STATUS.json` | machine truth, derived only from files on disk. Regenerated every tick |
| `PROGRESS.md` | the human surface, rendered **only** from `STATUS.json` |

**The presenter may not re-derive a single fact.** It reads the object and narrates it. This is the
same rule as Grillin's reader model — the thing that narrates is not allowed to go and look for
itself, because sooner or later it will narrate something that is not true.

`PROGRESS.md` leads with **⏸ Waiting on you** when any task needs a human, then **✗ Said done, gate
disagreed**, then every task, then what changed this tick. Someone who has been away opens one file
and knows where the plan is.

### The presenter agent, if you want prose

Optionally, one pane runs a presenter agent whose entire contract is:

- it reads `STATUS.json` and nothing else — not the tasks, not the findings, not the transcripts;
- it writes `PROGRESS-NOTES.md`, never `PROGRESS.md`, which is generated;
- it never says a task is done. It may only repeat `verified` or `claimed, ungated` as given.

An agent that summarises the work instead of the status will eventually report a claim as a fact,
which is the exact failure the receipt/verdict split exists to prevent.

